DAY 24 – File Handling in Python
📂 What I Learned Today

File handling in Python allows us to read from and write to files, which is essential for storing data permanently in real-world applications like logs, reports, and records.

📁 Why File Handling?

Store user data permanently

Read data from files (marks, records, notes)

Used in real applications like:

Address books

Billing systems

Exam report generators

📌 File Modes in Python
Mode	Meaning	Use Case
r	Read (default)	Read existing file
w	Write	Overwrites file
a	Append	Add content to end
x	Create	Create new file
b	Binary	Images, videos
t	Text (default)	Text files
✅ Opening a File
file = open("students.txt", "r")

✅ Reading From a File
1️⃣ read() – Read entire file
file = open("notes.txt", "r")
print(file.read())
file.close()

2️⃣ readline() – Read one line
file = open("notes.txt", "r")
print(file.readline())
file.close()

3️⃣ readlines() – Read all lines as list
file = open("notes.txt", "r")
lines = file.readlines()
print(lines)
file.close()

✅ Writing to a File (Overwrite)
file = open("data.txt", "w")
file.write("Namaskara Bengaluru!\n")
file.write("Python is awesome!")
file.close()


📌 Old content will be removed

✅ Appending to a File
file = open("data.txt", "a")
file.write("\nThis line is added later.")
file.close()


📌 Old content is preserved

✅ Best Practice – Using with
with open("students.txt", "r") as file:
    content = file.read()
    print(content)


✔ Automatically closes the file
✔ Clean and professional

🗃️ Writing List Data to File
students = ["Ravi", "Meena", "Dinesh"]

with open("students.txt", "w") as file:
    for student in students:
        file.write(student + "\n")

📝 Reading File Line by Line
with open("students.txt", "r") as file:
    for line in file:
        print("Student:", line.strip())


.strip() removes \n

❌ Common Errors
Error	Cause
FileNotFoundError	File doesn’t exist
PermissionError	Access denied
IsADirectoryError	Folder opened as file
